### How do you reverse an array in-place with two pointers?

```java
package com.bank.util;

import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.stream.IntStream;

public class ReverseArrayDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] cheques = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};

        // Two-pointer in-place reversal
        int left = 0;
        int right = cheques.length - 1;
        while (left < right) {
            int temp = cheques[left];
            cheques[left] = cheques[right];
            cheques[right] = temp;
            left++;
            right--;
        }

        System.out.println("Reversed: " + Arrays.toString(cheques));
    }
}
```

```text
Reversed: [5, 4, 3, 2, 1]
```

### How do you reverse using streams?

```java
int[] original = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
int n = original.length;

// Stream approach: new array, source unchanged
int[] reversed = IntStream.rangeClosed(1, n)
    .map(i -> original[n - i])
    .toArray();

System.out.println("Original: " + Arrays.toString(original));
System.out.println("Reversed: " + Arrays.toString(reversed));
```

```text
Original: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
Reversed: [5, 4, 3, 2, 1]
```

The stream approach produces a NEW array. The original is not mutated.

### How do the two approaches compare?

| Feature | Two-pointer | Stream |
|---------|------------|--------|
| Time | O(n) | O(n) |
| Space | O(1) in-place | O(n) new array |
| Mutates source | Yes | No |
| Readability | Imperative loop | Declarative pipeline |
| Thread safety | Unsafe (mutates) | Safe (immutable result) |

### What about reversing a list of strings?

```java
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Collections;

List<String> branches = List.of("Mumbai", "Delhi", "Chennai");

// Stream approach
List<String> reversed = IntStream.rangeClosed(1, branches.size())
    .mapToObj(i -> branches.get(branches.size() - i))
    .toList();
// [Chennai, Delhi, Mumbai]
```

### What are edge cases?

```java
// Empty array: no swaps needed
int[] empty = {};
// left=0, right=-1, loop does not execute

// Single element: no swap needed
int[] single = {42};
// left=0, right=0, left < right is false, loop does not execute

// Even-length: all elements swap
int[] even = {1, 2, 3, 4};
// Swap (1,4) then (2,3). Result: [4, 3, 2, 1]

// Odd-length: middle stays
int[] odd = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
// Swap (1,5) then (2,4). 3 stays. Result: [5, 4, 3, 2, 1]
```

### What about using a Stack?

```java
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

// Stack approach: push all, then pop all
int[] arr = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
for (int val : arr) stack.push(val);
for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) arr[i] = stack.pop();
// [5, 4, 3, 2, 1] - O(n) time, O(n) space
```

### What about Collections.reverse for lists?

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;

List<String> mutable = new ArrayList<>(List.of("Mumbai", "Delhi", "Chennai"));
Collections.reverse(mutable);
// [Chennai, Delhi, Mumbai]

// WRONG: List.of returns unmodifiable list
// Collections.reverse(List.of("A", "B")); // UnsupportedOperationException!
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- The stream approach using `IntStream.rangeClosed` avoids autoboxing because it stays in the `IntStream` pipeline and produces a primitive `int[]` via `toArray()`.
- In banking, the non-mutating stream approach is preferred when the original order is part of the audit trail. The ledger stores balances oldest-first, but the statement shows newest-first.
- `Collections.reverse(list)` reverses a list in place but only works on mutable lists. `List.of(...)` returns an unmodifiable list, so `Collections.reverse` throws `UnsupportedOperationException`.
